# SEC Filing Intelligence Assistant 📊

A production-grade Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) system that lets you ask 
natural-language questions over real SEC 10-K annual filings from Apple, Microsoft, 
and Tesla — and get back grounded, cited answers powered by Claude AI.

I built this project to develop hands-on RAG experience while job hunting after 
completing my Masters in October 2025. I'm currently doing a Coursera course on 
RAG and Agentic AI, and I wanted a real project to back that up rather than just 
a certificate on its own.

---

## What it does

You can ask questions like:
- *"What was Apple's total revenue in 2023?"*
- *"What are Microsoft's main cloud products?"*
- *"How did Tesla's automotive revenue change between 2023 and 2024?"*

And get back accurate, cited answers pulled directly from the actual SEC filings — 
not from the model's training memory.

---

## How it works

```
SEC 10-K Filings (Apple, Microsoft, Tesla)
        ↓
HTML Cleaning → Text Chunking (1000 chars / 200 overlap)
        ↓
Sentence Transformer Embeddings → ChromaDB Vector Store
        ↓
User Question → Vector Similarity Search → Top 5 Relevant Chunks
        ↓
Claude API reads chunks → Cited Answer with Source Attribution
```

---

## RAGAS Evaluation Scores

One of the key parts of this project was not just building the RAG pipeline but 
actually measuring how good it is. I used RAGAS — an open source evaluation 
framework — to score the system across 4 metrics:

| Metric | Score | What it means |
|---|---|---|
| Faithfulness | 1.000 | Claude never hallucinated — every fact came from the actual filings |
| Answer Relevancy | 0.890 | Answers were highly relevant to the questions asked |
| Context Precision | 0.815 | Most retrieved chunks were genuinely useful |
| Context Recall | 1.000 | All the information needed to answer was successfully retrieved |

---

## Tech Stack

| Tool | Purpose |
|---|---|
| LangChain | Orchestration framework |
| ChromaDB | Local vector database |
| Claude API (Anthropic) | Answer generation |
| all-MiniLM-L6-v2 | Sentence transformer embeddings |
| RAGAS | Evaluation framework |
| Streamlit | Interactive frontend |
| BeautifulSoup | HTML cleaning for SEC filings |

---

## Challenges I ran into

This wasn't a smooth build — a few things that took real debugging:

- **M1 Mac dependency conflicts** — PyTorch 2.4+ requires Python 3.11 but several 
  other libraries needed 3.10, so I had to carefully pin versions to get everything 
  working together
- **Raw HTML in SEC filings** — the 10-K filings are stored as HTML files, so the 
  first version of the retriever was returning chunks full of CSS and div tags. 
  I added a BeautifulSoup cleaning step that stripped all markup before chunking, 
  which significantly improved answer quality
- **NumPy version conflict** — the latest NumPy (2.x) broke several ML libraries 
  that were compiled against NumPy 1.x, requiring a downgrade to get the embedding 
  pipeline stable
- **RAGAS API changes** — RAGAS had breaking changes between versions during 
  development, requiring adjustments to how the LLM and embeddings were wired in

---

## Project Structure

```
rag-document-assistant/
├── src/
│   ├── ingest.py        # Loads, cleans, chunks and embeds SEC filings into ChromaDB
│   ├── retriever.py     # Vector similarity search over the embedded chunks
│   ├── generator.py     # Sends retrieved chunks + question to Claude, returns cited answer
│   └── evaluate.py      # RAGAS evaluation framework — scores the pipeline on 4 metrics
├── app.py               # Streamlit frontend with chat interface and source viewer
├── .env.example         # Template for required environment variables
├── requirements.txt     # All Python dependencies
└── .gitignore           # Excludes API keys, venv, and large data files
```

---

## Setup

### 1. Clone the repo
```bash
git clone https://github.com/PranayCh-Sai/RAG-Document-Assistant.git
cd RAG-Document-Assistant
```

### 2. Create a virtual environment
```bash
python3.10 -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

### 3. Add your API key
```bash
cp .env.example .env
# Open .env and add your Anthropic API key
```

### 4. Download and ingest SEC filings
```bash
python3.10 src/ingest.py
```
This downloads 10-K filings for Apple, Microsoft and Tesla, cleans the HTML, 
chunks the text and embeds everything into ChromaDB. Takes 20-40 minutes on 
first run.

### 5. Run the app
```bash
streamlit run app.py
```

---

## Future Plans

- **Add more companies** — expand beyond Apple, Microsoft and Tesla to cover 
  more of the S&P 500, making the system genuinely useful for financial research
- **Reranking** — integrate FlashRank or Cohere Rerank to reorder retrieved 
  chunks by relevance before passing to Claude, which should push Context 
  Precision above 0.85
- **Hybrid search** — combine vector similarity search with keyword search (BM25) 
  for better retrieval on specific financial terms like ticker symbols and exact 
  revenue figures
- **Multi-document comparison** — allow users to ask comparative questions across 
  companies, e.g. *"How does Apple's R&D spend compare to Microsoft's?"*
- **Deploy to cloud** — host the Streamlit app on a platform like Render or 
  Streamlit Cloud so it's publicly accessible without a local setup

